WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:12.190
In the 1950s, scientists programmed a first-generation
computer to learn using the same rules as

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:18.840
the human brain.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.390
This model is called a neural network.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:27.200
It’s made up of many basic units that compute
by taking inputs from other units and passing

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.760
them on as simple calculations.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.339
The answers are recorded by designated output
units.

00:00:33.339 --> 00:00:37.890
Pathways strengthen, and the model learns,
when many different inputs result in the same

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.960
output.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.210
Today, advanced versions of these models,
called deep neural networks, are the most

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.350
successful AI ever created.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:53.609
They power image and speech recognition and
recognize patterns in massive sets of data,

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:57.180
making astonishing predictions about the future.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:03.270
But there’s a dark mystery here: We have
no idea how deep neural networks work.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:08.399
What happens inside their billions of hidden
layers that allows them to converge to a solution?

00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:14.500
Now, for the first time ever, a group of researchers
has found a mathematical key that might just

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.130
open this black box.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:27.170
Historically, a lot of methods in machine
learning were designed to have simple properties,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:36.189
where the model designer was guaranteed that
they would obtain a certain solution if they

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:38.640
used a particular algorithm or model.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.560
Deep neural networks move away from that paradigm.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.661
The model designer chooses how many units
they would like to have in each one of the

00:01:46.661 --> 00:01:47.690
hidden layers.

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:52.280
That’s often referred to as the width of
the neural network.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.860
Yasaman Bahri and her colleagues at Google’s
Brain Team hoped to mathematically simplify

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:04.329
a deep neural network by considering an extreme
case: They took the networks’ width to be

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:05.329
infinite.

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:12.150
One of the goals was to understand what happens
in that limit of infinitely wide, deep neural

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.799
networks to see if we could say anything concrete
about it.

00:02:16.799 --> 00:02:18.760
Incredibly, they could.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:24.699
Bahri mathematically reduced those infinite-width
networks to something simpler called kernel

00:02:24.699 --> 00:02:30.260
machines, algorithms with a history going
back to the 19th century mathematician Carl

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.860
Friedrich Gauss.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.919
A kernel method captures a richness that is
similar to a deep neural network in terms

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:42.290
of its functional dependence on the input,
but it's simpler than a deep neural network

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:47.970
because it's linear and its parameters and
neural networks in general are non-linear.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:53.580
Kernel machines find patterns in data by projecting
the data into extremely high dimensions.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:58.950
They map each point on a low-dimensional data
set to a point in a higher dimension, and

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:04.170
then use the data point’s characteristics
to identify a class it belongs to.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:10.220
They do this classification using a geometric
object called a hyperplane.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.600
Kernel machines can compute infinite-dimensional
data while staying firmly in the realm of

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.919
lower dimensional space.

00:03:15.919 --> 00:03:20.829
Bahri and her team were able to show that
kernel methods are mathematically equivalent

00:03:20.829 --> 00:03:26.250
to an idealized version of a deep net–the
first major step to cracking open the black

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.960
box of deep learning.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:35.780
It's very rare to have an exact equivalence
between two things, in particular if that

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.040
other thing is exactly solvable.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:41.650
You can write down the expressions mathematically.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.770
From my perspective, that's quite appealing.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:49.770
If this equivalence can be extended beyond
idealized neural networks, it may explain

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:55.380
how practical deep neural networks achieve
their astonishing results.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.540
To describe finite width networks, more work
is needed, but at least if you have an exactly

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.340
solvable model where you have that full handle,
you can then start to be systematic about

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.500
what's missing.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:13.060
And so from my mind, that gives you a starting
point.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:20.199
There is a great debate happening in the world
of set theory, the study of collections of

00:04:20.199 --> 00:04:22.940
numbers and other mathematical objects.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.660
It’s about the nature of infinity – or,
rather, infinities.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:35.070
The remarkable thing about infinities is that
they come in different sizes.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.600
Take two infinite sets of numbers.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.060
If every number from one set can be paired
to a number from another, the sets are said

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.570
to be bijective, or the same size.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:49.460
So it's not just the case that all infinite
sets are bijective with each other, that there's

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.270
a one-to-one correspondence between them.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:56.070
You have actually lots of examples of sets
such that one is strictly smaller than the

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.070
other.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:02.000
These questions about the sizes of infinities
go back nearly 150 years, when the German

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.370
mathematician Georg Cantor rocked the mathematical
world by discovering something that seems

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.780
counterintuitive: The infinite set of real
numbers, which includes every point on the

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:17.180
number line, outnumbers natural numbers – even
though there are infinitely many of both.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.180
After all, you can try to pair up these sets,
but you would have skipped over an infinite

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:19.180
amount of real numbers between them.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.340
In other words, these infinities are not equal.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:27.230
The smaller one’s size, or cardinality,
was classified as aleph-zero, and the next

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:30.590
biggest size is aleph-one.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:35.280
Cantor conjectured that this aleph-one is
exactly the size of the continuum of real

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.380
numbers.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:42.350
In other words, there are no sizes of infinity
between the sets of natural and real numbers.

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:46.130
This came to be known as the continuum hypothesis.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.600
But he could never prove it.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:54.140
The axioms, or basic foundations of mathematics,
simply didn’t govern things like Cantor’s

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:55.700
infinity problem.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:01.250
But this year, set theorist David Aspero and
his longtime collaborator Ralf Schindler got

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.389
closer to finding the answer anyway.

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:11.500
We had the right scenario in place about 10
years ago, but there was a missing ingredient.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.300
And then it just came, just by chance, quite
randomly.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:20.320
The breakthrough came when Asperó used a
technique, known as forcing, to create a mathematical

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.660
object called a witness.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:29.210
He used this witness to verify that an extremely
powerful axiom, Martin’s maximum plus plus,

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:33.350
actually implies a rival axiom, star.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:38.620
By unifying the two rival axioms, Asperó
and Schindler showed that they are both likely

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:44.440
true – implying that an extra size of infinity
actually does sit between the sets of natural

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:46.380
and real numbers.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.720
This would prove that there are more reals
than Cantor had thought, finally bringing

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:58.480
closure to the 150-year-old mystery and offering
a coherent alternative to the continuum hypothesis.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.889
But this is not even close to the end of the
story.

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:07.620
Newer and stronger axioms are already challenging
this result and a battle is underway to decide

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.720
which side is right.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:14.510
The final chapter on the actual number of
real numbers – the true size of the continuum

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:21.620
– is yet to be written.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.620
This is a Liouville field–or, at least,
an idea of it.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.520
It’s a wildly chaotic mathematical surface
where the height of every point is chosen

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.390
at random.

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:37.510
Forty years ago, the theoretical physicist
Alexander Polyakov found a striking use for

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:41.020
these fields: as a model of quantum physics.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:45.889
Polyakov intuited that they could do this
by unlocking the behavior of theoretical objects

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:50.889
called strings, and building a simplified
model of quantum gravity in two dimensions.

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:57.210
It can be seen as a toy model for higher-dimensional
cases, but it can also be seen as string theory,

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:01.760
because string theory in some sense is about
two- dimensional surfaces.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.810
But Polyakov’s formula for understanding
the Liouville field stopped tantalizingly

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.790
short of being rigorous.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:13.420
Although he attempted to explain it using
a path integral developed by Richard Feynman,

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:17.860
the Liouville field resisted being described
in this way.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:23.740
Around the year 2013, we started looking at
Polyakov’s path integral and according to

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:28.130
what physicists were saying, if we could make
sense of this path integral, we could directly

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:31.050
construct in the continuum quantum gravity.

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:34.510
So we took the path integral and we said,
“Okay, let's give it a shot.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.519
We're going to try to define it.”

00:08:36.519 --> 00:08:42.210
Mathematician Vincent Vargas and his colleagues
set out to precisely describe Polyakov’s

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:46.830
path integral using a different approach:
probability theory.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:52.620
They began by transforming the Liouville field
into a far milder object called a Gaussian

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:53.620
free field.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:58.460
It's kind of this very rough landscape where
there are infinite spikes everywhere.

00:08:58.460 --> 00:09:03.420
From the point of view of physics, the Gaussian
free field is a boring theory.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.670
It's a trivial theory where everything is
computable straight away.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:12.360
And what we realized and which came as a surprise,
I think, to the physics community, is that

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:18.730
we could express anything that you naturally
want to compute on this theory just in terms

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.650
of something on the Gaussian free field.

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:23.830
There were other leads to build on as well.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:29.490
In 1984, as a potential workaround to his
failed path integral, Polyakov began trying

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:34.900
to develop a technique called the bootstrap
— a mathematical ladder that gradually builds

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:38.740
up to a full, complex representation of a
field.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:44.920
By chance, two unrelated pairs of physicists
in the 1990s built on Polyakov’s bootstrap

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:48.120
and managed to completely solve the Liouville
field.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.010
They called their formula “DOZZ."

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:58.820
Even though it seemed to work, it was a miraculously
lucky guess, and they couldn’t prove it.

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:07.790
This formula, which comes out of a black box–I
mean, it's black magic–well, it actually

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.070
has a probabilistic meaning.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:18.140
So what we did is show that our path integral
probability construction is equivalent, completely

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:22.930
equivalent to that bootstrap construction.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:27.970
Last year, they unveiled a new and improved
version of Polyakov’s path integral, defined

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:31.050
in terms of the Gaussian free field.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:36.620
The work also explains the mysterious origins
of the DOZZ formula.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:41.730
We're mapping probability theory to the bootstrap,
or, if I were just talking on the mathematical

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:46.360
side, we’re mapping probability theory to
representation theory.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:52.210
And with that, they proved that the Liouville
field models quantum gravity, exactly as Polyakov

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:55.140
thought it would 40 years ago.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:03.110
By bridging these two fields of math which
are really distinct, a priori it enables us

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:18.950
to compute things that physicists don't know
how to compute.

